WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.150
- Oh, those shoes are great.

00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:03.360
- Oh, thanks!

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.060
- So wait, this is cool that
you're actually put out,

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.070
is it your own type of violin?

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:08.271
- Yes.

00:00:08.271 --> 00:00:09.540
- So wait, what makes
your violin different?

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.130
- Well, so through the years
I've learned things I like

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:14.630
and now at this point I
customize all my violins

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:18.000
because classical violins
are very hard to tune.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:18.920
And especially if you're in

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.330
the middle of a show or a concert-

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.100
- So you tune, like
while you're in a show?

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.670
- Yeah, the pegs will slip
and it's really hard to get.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:28.220
So I've made these violins
that have these special pegs

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:32.000
on them that allow you to very
quickly adjust in a second.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.850
I also put some custom
artwork that I actually drew

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:36.670
and it's etched into the violin,

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:38.787
I'm great at stick figures, so this-

00:00:38.787 --> 00:00:40.870
- Oh, that's actually really cool.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:43.010
- That's the, well
that's the original one,

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.320
but it's much cuter on the violin.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:46.561
It's like gold and nice, but yeah,

00:00:46.561 --> 00:00:47.394
- I love that

00:00:47.394 --> 00:00:48.557
- It's just a really special piece

00:00:48.557 --> 00:00:51.220
and it's a beautiful sounding violin.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.500
So I was really happy, and I
was so excited our first round,

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.610
you know, sold out immediately

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.570
and we just barely got a new round in.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:58.750
So I'm excited about that.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:00:59.583
- That's great!

00:00:59.583 --> 00:01:00.705
- Yeah.

00:01:00.705 --> 00:01:01.598
- That's awesome, and think of all the,

00:01:01.598 --> 00:01:03.064
like kids you're inspiring,

00:01:03.064 --> 00:01:04.490
especially to start playing
violin, that's so cool.

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:05.890
Yeah, you're very powerful in that sense,

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:07.465
that's really cool.

00:01:07.465 --> 00:01:08.298
- Thank you.

00:01:08.298 --> 00:01:10.370
I have a question and
this is, I just need to

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:11.203
know this answer.

00:01:11.203 --> 00:01:12.363
- Okay.

00:01:12.363 --> 00:01:15.010
- Okay. So Shelton and I on
The Voice have often argued

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.200
the difference between
a fiddle and a violin.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.590
So what do you think the difference

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:20.760
between a fiddle and a violin is?

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.630
- It's the way it's played.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:23.750
- Totally.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:24.788
- Yeah.

00:01:24.788 --> 00:01:25.949
- So we're gonna edit this out to where-

00:01:25.949 --> 00:01:26.782
(Lindsey laughs)

00:01:26.782 --> 00:01:27.797
to where,

00:01:27.797 --> 00:01:29.853
- [Lindsey] I was gonna
say I have a feeling

00:01:29.853 --> 00:01:30.686
that Blake would know that-

00:01:30.686 --> 00:01:31.643
- Here's the thing: No, but I feel like

00:01:31.643 --> 00:01:33.810
they also use different strings.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:36.060
Don't fiddle players usually
like, is it steel strings?

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.890
Or like, I feel like they use different,

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.210
I thought I was gonna be
right on this, so I asked,

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:41.043
- [Audience Member] It's a good question.

00:01:41.043 --> 00:01:43.810
- It's a great, I get that
asked that all the time

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:45.790
but like sometimes I'll play
fiddle music and sometimes-

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:46.910
- [Kelly] It's how you play it.

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:47.920
- It's how you play.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:48.877
- [Kelly] So you're saying he was right.

00:01:48.877 --> 00:01:49.710
It's fine.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:50.713
- I'm sorry, Kelly.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.138
- We're gonna edit this out.

00:01:54.138 --> 00:01:55.620
- Yeah.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:56.920
- [Kelly] I'm just
kidding, I'll keep it in.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.010
No, you also run a nonprofit,
this is really cool.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.020
Cause I love when people do so well

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:01.970
that you help others as well.

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:03.810
So this is called The Upside Fund?

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:04.643
- Yes.

00:02:04.643 --> 00:02:05.476
- What is it about, what
do you do with that?

00:02:05.476 --> 00:02:08.080
- So just because my
own personal experience

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.100
I've lost several people that were some

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.440
of the closest people in my life

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.490
my best friend and my dad
both passed away from cancer.

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:18.510
And I experience firsthand
so much time in the hospital

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.950
seeing how it's run, seeing
how medical bills pile up.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:25.860
And most people sometimes get
trapped under these forever.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.720
And, and especially in my
case, we lost those people.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.720
And so I started The Upside
Fund to help families going

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.410
through medical crisis and
help pay those medical bills.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.460
And it's something that I've been doing,

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:39.050
it started as just like a
Christmas giveaway one year,

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:41.840
I was like, email me if you know

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:42.710
someone that's going through it,

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:44.550
and I made a separate
email and I got thousands

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.760
of emails and people that
couldn't pay their medical bills.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.690
And it made me realize
there is such a need here.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:52.726
And so I, I turned it
into official fund and

00:02:52.726 --> 00:02:53.559
- [Kelly] Yeah

00:02:53.559 --> 00:02:54.720
you know, I've been doing
it for four years now.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.030
- [Kelly] Honestly,
that's the coolest thing.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:58.610
You're so talented, you do so many things.

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.330
It's the coolest thing
you're doing to though

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:04.560
because I have this talk,
argument with people all the time

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.200
because I feel like it's very hard,

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.720
like cuz I'm from a poor background

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:11.000
and it's very hard for
poor people to stay well.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.310
Like cuz it's so expensive.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:13.960
The medical field it's
so expensive and yes,

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:15.070
all the advances are wonderful

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:16.280
and what we can do for people.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:17.900
But I feel like sometimes
it's only available

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:19.250
for people that can afford it.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:21.270
And it's, and it's really
cool that you're doing that

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.590
because people sometimes don't
take their family members

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:25.940
in cuz they know they're,
they're gonna get stuck with,

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:27.920
I'm talking hundreds of thousands

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.000
of dollars and it's, nobody can,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.350
nobody is ever gonna be
able to pay that off.

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:34.230
So that's so cool that you're doing that.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:35.063
- Yeah.

00:03:35.063 --> 00:03:35.896
- [Kelly] I can only imagine

00:03:35.896 --> 00:03:39.050
the families that you're helping.
It's a really cool thing.

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:40.780
I know that you love helping others.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:43.270
And we have a girl here who
wants to be just like you

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:45.580
and I know you you've
seen her right? Karolina?

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:46.555
- I have.

00:03:46.555 --> 00:03:48.340
- [Kelly] Okay, Karolina's
videos are amazing,

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.240
I cannot wait for her to meet you.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:51.942
I'm very excited about this moment.

00:03:51.942 --> 00:03:54.800
So Karolina has no idea Lindsey's here.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:57.390
In fact, she thinks she's
on the show just to perform

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:58.660
one of Lindsey's songs, which we would've

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:00.000
had her just do that as well.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.100
But this is cooler.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:02.870
So Lindsay's gonna go hide,

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:05.020
and when we come back, we're
gonna surprise her big time.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:06.450
And I hope she's not so nervous.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:07.283
We are back!

00:04:07.283 --> 00:04:09.824
And at 13 years old, our
next guest is a violin

00:04:09.824 --> 00:04:13.110
virtuoso with big dreams,
and she's working hard

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:14.760
to achieve them, busking weekly

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.190
here in Los Angeles and creating

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:18.741
a YouTube channel. Her
videos have been viewed,

00:04:18.741 --> 00:04:23.741
this is a real number people,
more than 2 billion times.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.930
She's gonna perform in just a minute.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:28.117
But first let's meet Karolina Protsenko!

00:04:29.066 --> 00:04:31.065
Thank you so much for coming Karolina.

00:04:31.065 --> 00:04:33.740
- Thank you for having me,
it's an honor to be here.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.370
- Oh my god, you're so gifted.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:36.940
How did you get into the violin?

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:39.409
Did you want, was that
like right off the bat?

00:04:39.409 --> 00:04:41.920
- I was born in a musical family,

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:42.753
- [Kelly] Oh!

00:04:42.753 --> 00:04:45.110
- all the time I was
surrounded with music.

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:48.320
My mom plays piano and
she sings beautifully.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:49.530
My dad plays guitar.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:51.070
And when I was six years old

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:53.860
I really wanted to play an instrument.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:55.229
- Yeah.

00:04:55.229 --> 00:04:56.062
- So my parents asked me

00:04:56.062 --> 00:04:58.530
do you wanna play saxophone or violin?

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.390
And I chose violin.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:02.730
And you know, when you're six years old

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:04.790
you don't think why you're choosing it.

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:07.073
You like it and you start playing it.

00:05:07.073 --> 00:05:08.552
(Audience affectionately sighs)

00:05:08.552 --> 00:05:10.830
So yeah, I started practicing every day

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:12.940
and it became my passion.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:17.940
And I learned more than
250 songs on violin by ear.

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:20.170
- Wow. By ear, that's amazing!

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:24.010
- Yes. I don't use music
sheets and every week

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:26.390
I learn two more songs to perform.

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:28.490
- Wow. That's incredible.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:30.380
You also should do like voice-over work.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:32.231
Your voice is so cute and beautiful.

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:33.064
- Thank you so much.

00:05:33.064 --> 00:05:33.897
- I was like, you started talking and

00:05:33.897 --> 00:05:35.570
all of the sudden I was
watching an animated film.

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:36.870
You have the most beautiful voice.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:38.440
So did you start, cuz
you're playing violin

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:39.870
but then all the sudden you
wanted to start dancing too,

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:41.263
so you took like dance classes as well?

00:05:41.263 --> 00:05:42.180
- Ballet, yeah.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:43.740
- That's fun. I love that you were like,

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.839
this just isn't enough, I'm gonna do more

00:05:45.839 --> 00:05:47.750
as if it wasn't already enough,

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:48.980
but what's your ultimate goal?

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:50.180
Like musically speaking.

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:54.660
- I wanna travel the world with
my concerts, playing violin

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:59.080
and moving in costumes
and reaching more people.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.400
Also, I wanna write my own music.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:03.900
- That's amazing! Well, since you play

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.199
by ear, that, I think you'd
probably write really well.

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:07.940
Do you have like someone

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:10.556
in the industry that
you kind of look up to?

00:06:10.556 --> 00:06:12.830
- Yes. When I was six years old

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:17.280
I found a video on YouTube
of Lindsey Stirling

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:18.400
performing, and-

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:19.860
- I've had her on here before.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:20.693
- Really?

00:06:20.693 --> 00:06:21.553
- Yeah, yeah. I've actually
interviewed her before

00:06:21.553 --> 00:06:24.810
- Wow, that's awesome! But
she wasn't just playing

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.092
as many violinists, she was also dancing,

00:06:27.092 --> 00:06:29.410
and I wasn't dancing at that time.

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:31.350
So I tried to take ballet

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:33.610
and dance to incorporate it like her.

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:37.270
And what I really love
about her is that every

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:38.377
video she has, she,

00:06:38.377 --> 00:06:41.360
she heals someone or makes someone happy.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:44.560
That's the, the thought that she brings

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:47.030
and the message and it
really warms my heart.

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:48.516
And I also went to-

00:06:48.516 --> 00:06:49.405
- I love you.

00:06:49.405 --> 00:06:50.238
- a concert,

00:06:50.238 --> 00:06:53.370
- Keep going. You are making
me so happy right now.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:54.203
- Thank you

00:06:54.203 --> 00:06:55.630
- Oh my gosh. So you went to her concert?

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:58.590
- Yeah. My dad made me a
surprise when I was eight.

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:00.020
It was a Christmas concert.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.610
I even made her a Christmas card.

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:05.822
And at the end of the song,
me and my dad were running

00:07:05.822 --> 00:07:08.640
